Before any flips are revealed, the expected number of heads is 8 × (0.5 × 0.1 + 0.5 × 0.5) = 2.4, so the fair value is 12.

The settlement can land anywhere from about 0 to 30. Past trades suggest a fair share of informed counterparties. So I quote a wide spread around 12: 7 below and 7 above.

{"bid": 5, "ask": 19}

The expected sum of two cards is 2 × 99/13 ≈ 15.2. The possible sums cluster at 2, 7, 10, 13, 15, 18, 21 and 24.

If alpha is high, tight quotes get picked off by informed traders. Quoting 10 / 21 means informed counterparties only trade when the sum is 2, 7 (they sell) or 24 (they buy). Their expected cost is about 0.75 per quote. Noise traders, whose valuations spread around 15.2, still give about 0.86 per quote in expected edge. Weighting by the prior on alpha, this comes out slightly positive.

{"bid": 10, "ask": 21}

The prior mean of the max is about 9.44. In the first quote of this round (bid 7.8, ask 11) nobody traded. That is mildly informative: an informed trader would have stayed out only if the value was 8, 10 or 11. So I lean towards a fair value of about 9.6–9.8, with less weight on 12 and on values below 8.

The history points to a meaningful informed share. Every round settled at 11, and several buys came at asks below 11. An ask of 11 is safe from informed buyers unless the value is 12, and noise buyers still lift it with decent probability. A bid around 8.2 limits losses to informed sellers to the cases where the value is 8 or less, while still catching low noise valuations.

